  "summary": "Single-molecule footprinting and WGBS datasets now let sequence-based models resolve chromatin states beyond bulk averages.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is predicting regulatory state from DNA sequence at finer resolution than bulk signal. ChAI-Net predicts locus-specific chromatin co-accessibility from DNA sequence using single-molecule footprinting data. Mola and S2d predict single-molecule chromatin accessibility from sequence as distributions. FiberBrowser and FiberHMM convert diverse footprinting datasets into per-molecule chromatin states. CNN-CR learns compact shared promoter motif families that predict expression across 116 human biosamples and are interpreted differently by cell type. Other items apply structured latent or probabilistic models to non-sequence layers: HiCONA for hierarchical 3D genome inheritance, C-Fetch for contact-map features, and a Bayesian HMM segmentome of WGBS methylomes. The canine k-mer study is a looser fit. What is new is the emphasis on distributions over single molecules and compact, reusable representations.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
